  "records": [
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2025-24-43",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "bca8a6a7a2d2d91208b21b521888440cb77d5f878c9f758bcee8ea2a7c92f372",
      "evidence_id": "ev-bca8a6a7a2d2-0000-0003",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 2,
        "start": 2
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The above-entitled matter came on for oral argument before the Supreme Court of the United States at 11:59 a.m."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Michael R. Williams",
      "case_id": "2025-24-43",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "bca8a6a7a2d2d91208b21b521888440cb77d5f878c9f758bcee8ea2a7c92f372",
      "evidence_id": "ev-bca8a6a7a2d2-0000-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 5,
        "start": 5
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "States have long assigned students to sports teams by sex. West Virginia is no different. Maintaining separate boys' and girls' sports teams ensures that girls can safely and fairly compete in school sports."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Joshua A. Block",
      "case_id": "2025-24-43",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "bca8a6a7a2d2d91208b21b521888440cb77d5f878c9f758bcee8ea2a7c92f372",
      "evidence_id": "ev-bca8a6a7a2d2-0000-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 6,
        "start": 6
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "schools can no longer designate teams by looking to biological sex. Instead, schools must place students on sports teams based on their self-identified gender. But that idea turns Title IX, a law Congress passed to protect educational opportunities for girls, into a law that actually denies those opportunities for girls."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Thomas",
      "case_id": "2025-24-43",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "bca8a6a7a2d2d91208b21b521888440cb77d5f878c9f758bcee8ea2a7c92f372",
      "evidence_id": "ev-bca8a6a7a2d2-0000-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 7,
        "start": 7
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE THOMAS: What's your view of what Title IX -- how it defined the separated sexes, male and female?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Sotomayor",
      "case_id": "2025-24-43",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "bca8a6a7a2d2d91208b21b521888440cb77d5f878c9f758bcee8ea2a7c92f372",
      "evidence_id": "ev-bca8a6a7a2d2-0001-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 25,
        "start": 25
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SOTOMAYOR: What do you --"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kavanaugh",
      "case_id": "2025-24-43",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "bca8a6a7a2d2d91208b21b521888440cb77d5f878c9f758bcee8ea2a7c92f372",
      "evidence_id": "ev-bca8a6a7a2d2-0001-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 25,
        "start": 25
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE KAVANAUGH: And what about Bostock?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Williams",
      "case_id": "2025-24-43",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "bca8a6a7a2d2d91208b21b521888440cb77d5f878c9f758bcee8ea2a7c92f372",
      "evidence_id": "ev-bca8a6a7a2d2-0001-0002",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 26,
        "start": 26
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "I think the reason why is, well, to be clear, our -- our statute is very different from a specific choice to say a transgender person shall not participate because of their transgender status."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2025-24-43",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "bca8a6a7a2d2d91208b21b521888440cb77d5f878c9f758bcee8ea2a7c92f372",
      "evidence_id": "ev-bca8a6a7a2d2-0001-0003",
      "kind": "fact",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 26,
        "start": 26
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Both statutes use the term \"sex.\""
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Mooppan",
      "case_id": "2025-24-43",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "bca8a6a7a2d2d91208b21b521888440cb77d5f878c9f758bcee8ea2a7c92f372",
      "evidence_id": "ev-bca8a6a7a2d2-0001-0004",
      "kind": "amicus_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 44,
        "start": 44
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. MOOPPAN: Mr. Chief Justice, and may it please the Court: So there have been a lot of different arguments made this morning, and so I think it might be helpful to just focus on what I think are the easiest way to resolve both of the claims in this case."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2025-24-43",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "bca8a6a7a2d2d91208b21b521888440cb77d5f878c9f758bcee8ea2a7c92f372",
      "evidence_id": "ev-bca8a6a7a2d2-0001-0005",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 44,
        "start": 44
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "ORAL ARGUMENT OF HASHIM M. MOOPPAN FOR THE UNITED STATES, AS AMICUS CURIAE SUPPORTING THE PETITIONERS"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Mooppan",
      "case_id": "2025-24-43",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "bca8a6a7a2d2d91208b21b521888440cb77d5f878c9f758bcee8ea2a7c92f372",
      "evidence_id": "ev-bca8a6a7a2d2-0002-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 47,
        "start": 47
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "the argument we're making in those cases is the statute and the regs allow separation based on sex because of the biological differences between men and women. If you purport to separate based on biological sex, but then you allow some biological males to play on the female team, you've undermined the justification for separating in the first place."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Mooppan",
      "case_id": "2025-24-43",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "bca8a6a7a2d2d91208b21b521888440cb77d5f878c9f758bcee8ea2a7c92f372",
      "evidence_id": "ev-bca8a6a7a2d2-0002-0002",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 52,
        "start": 52
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. MOOPPAN: Well, locker rooms and showers aren't covered by any of the things you just identified, not the living facilities provision, not the Javits Amendment. None of those things covered locker rooms and showers. So, unless you use a similarly situated requirement, you would to have say that Title IX bans single-sex locker rooms and showers."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kavanaugh",
      "case_id": "2025-24-43",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "bca8a6a7a2d2d91208b21b521888440cb77d5f878c9f758bcee8ea2a7c92f372",
      "evidence_id": "ev-bca8a6a7a2d2-0002-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 60,
        "start": 60
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE KAVANAUGH: I just want to be crystal-clear about that. If we say sex in Title IX is biological sex and then we get to the next case, the California case or whatever it is, how would California still prevail if we've said that here?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Mooppan",
      "case_id": "2025-24-43",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "bca8a6a7a2d2d91208b21b521888440cb77d5f878c9f758bcee8ea2a7c92f372",
      "evidence_id": "ev-bca8a6a7a2d2-0002-0004",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 66,
        "start": 66
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. MOOPPAN: Because the law doesn't classify on the basis of transgender status. It classifies on the basis of sex, biological sex. Jus t like in Skrmetti, the law there classified on the basis of age and medical treatment, here, the law classifies on the basis of biological sex. The person's gender identity is wholly irrelevant to how the law applies."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Mooppan",
      "case_id": "2025-24-43",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "bca8a6a7a2d2d91208b21b521888440cb77d5f878c9f758bcee8ea2a7c92f372",
      "evidence_id": "ev-bca8a6a7a2d2-0002-0005",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 68,
        "start": 68
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. MOOPPAN: No. A, I don't think Title IX covers disparate impact even on the basis of sex. It certainly doesn't cover disparate impact on the basis of gender identity."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "West Virginia",
      "case_id": "2025-24-43",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "bca8a6a7a2d2d91208b21b521888440cb77d5f878c9f758bcee8ea2a7c92f372",
      "evidence_id": "ev-bca8a6a7a2d2-0003-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 69,
        "start": 69
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "West Virginia argues more generally that this Court's reasoning in Bostock does not apply to Title IX. To distinguish Title VII from Title IX, West Virginia argues that Title IX protects groups instead of individuals and applies only when sex is the sole cause of adverse treatment."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Thomas",
      "case_id": "2025-24-43",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "bca8a6a7a2d2d91208b21b521888440cb77d5f878c9f758bcee8ea2a7c92f372",
      "evidence_id": "ev-bca8a6a7a2d2-0003-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 70,
        "start": 70
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE THOMAS: Doesn't your claim ultimately depend on the existence of sex-segregated sports?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Block",
      "case_id": "2025-24-43",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "bca8a6a7a2d2d91208b21b521888440cb77d5f878c9f758bcee8ea2a7c92f372",
      "evidence_id": "ev-bca8a6a7a2d2-0003-0002",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 76,
        "start": 76
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "I think it is absolutely reasonable as applied to cisgender students. I think that as applied to transgender students, instead of providing them equal overall opportunity, it's a complete exclusion from the program. And so that -- so that's our argument, that it's reasonable as applied in the context of cisgender people, but interpreting the regulations to authorize this sort of categorical exclusion that doesn't give B.P.J. an equal set of opportunities to choose from would be an unreasonable way to implement Title IX."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Jackson",
      "case_id": "2025-24-43",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "bca8a6a7a2d2d91208b21b521888440cb77d5f878c9f758bcee8ea2a7c92f372",
      "evidence_id": "ev-bca8a6a7a2d2-0004-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 90,
        "start": 90
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE JACKSON: How -- how do you deal -- how -- how do you respond to or deal with the other side's characterization of that harm as just the disparate impact of this"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Block",
      "case_id": "2025-24-43",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "bca8a6a7a2d2d91208b21b521888440cb77d5f878c9f758bcee8ea2a7c92f372",
      "evidence_id": "ev-bca8a6a7a2d2-0004-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 91,
        "start": 91
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. BLOCK: Yeah. So -- so I guess I would say this. I think this Court has dealt -- dealt with the issue of things like constructive denials and constructive discharges in a variety of contexts. And under Title IX, an outright denial isn't required. Davis says a constructive denial also counts. And whenever there's a question of constructive denials, the Court applies a standard that's reasonable person in the plaintiff's position under all the circumstances."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Chief Justice Roberts",
      "case_id": "2025-24-43",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "bca8a6a7a2d2d91208b21b521888440cb77d5f878c9f758bcee8ea2a7c92f372",
      "evidence_id": "ev-bca8a6a7a2d2-0004-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 92,
        "start": 92
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "CHIEF JUSTICE ROBERTS: Well, I -- I'm sorry. You don't think we should have an operating definition of sex in Title IX?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Block",
      "case_id": "2025-24-43",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "bca8a6a7a2d2d91208b21b521888440cb77d5f878c9f758bcee8ea2a7c92f372",
      "evidence_id": "ev-bca8a6a7a2d2-0004-0003",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 93,
        "start": 93
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. BLOCK: Well, Your Honor, I guess I'd say I think Congress prohibited discrimination based on sex."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Chief Justice Roberts",
      "case_id": "2025-24-43",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "bca8a6a7a2d2d91208b21b521888440cb77d5f878c9f758bcee8ea2a7c92f372",
      "evidence_id": "ev-bca8a6a7a2d2-0004-0004",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 94,
        "start": 94
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "CHIEF JUSTICE ROBERTS: Well, related to sex. I -- I guess what you're saying is then we -- we do have to accept for your position that we're not dealing -- when -- when Congress says sex, we're not dealing with biological sex, but we're dealing with other characteristics that people might associate with sex?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Jackson",
      "case_id": "2025-24-43",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "bca8a6a7a2d2d91208b21b521888440cb77d5f878c9f758bcee8ea2a7c92f372",
      "evidence_id": "ev-bca8a6a7a2d2-0004-0006",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 96,
        "start": 96
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE JACKSON: So how does a Caban-type as-applied intermediate scrutiny analysis work in your view? It -- it is an under-developed area of the law."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kagan",
      "case_id": "2025-24-43",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "bca8a6a7a2d2d91208b21b521888440cb77d5f878c9f758bcee8ea2a7c92f372",
      "evidence_id": "ev-bca8a6a7a2d2-0004-0007",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 97,
        "start": 97
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE KAGAN: I -- I think a hard question that Ms. Hartnett got, maybe the hardest question on these -- on these lines was, if we recognize these sorts of as-applied challenges, doesn't that effectively turn intermediate scrutiny into strict scrutiny."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kagan",
      "case_id": "2025-24-43",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "bca8a6a7a2d2d91208b21b521888440cb77d5f878c9f758bcee8ea2a7c92f372",
      "evidence_id": "ev-bca8a6a7a2d2-0004-0008",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 98,
        "start": 98
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE KAGAN: What would your answer be to that? Would it be any different, or do you want to elaborate?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Williams",
      "case_id": "2025-24-43",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "bca8a6a7a2d2d91208b21b521888440cb77d5f878c9f758bcee8ea2a7c92f372",
      "evidence_id": "ev-bca8a6a7a2d2-0004-0010",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 101,
        "start": 101
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. WILLIAMS: I think you've now heard Respondent abandon the Fourth Circuit's logic on Title IX, and I think, in many ways, that makes this Court's task that much easier."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Source extraction",
      "case_id": "2025-24-43",
